? Why can't you write `static STATE: RefCell<u32> = RefCell::new(0);`?
- `RefCell::new` isn't a `const fn`
* Statics must be `Sync`, and `RefCell` allows unsynchronised mutation through `&`, so it is `!Sync`
- `RefCell` requires alloc
- Statics can't have interior mutability at all
> Every thread and interrupt handler can reach a static. Wrap it in something `Sync`: an atomic, or `critical_section::Mutex<RefCell<u32>>`.

? Which is the *only* primitive that makes mutation through a shared reference legal in Rust?
- `Cell<T>`
- `*mut T`
* `UnsafeCell<T>`
- `static mut`
> `Cell`, `RefCell`, atomics and mutexes are all built on `UnsafeCell`. Mutating through `&T` any other way is UB.

? Producer: `DATA.store(42, Relaxed); READY.store(true, X);` Consumer: `if READY.load(Y) { DATA.load(Relaxed) }`. Which (X, Y) guarantees the consumer sees 42?
- (Relaxed, Relaxed)
- (Acquire, Release)
* (Release, Acquire)
- (Relaxed, SeqCst)
> A Release store synchronises with an Acquire load that reads its value: all writes before the release are visible after the acquire.

? On a single-core MCU, main code holds a spin lock and an interrupt handler tries to take the same lock. What happens?
- The interrupt waits until main releases the lock
* Deadlock: the handler spins forever because main can't run until the handler returns
- The lock is automatically upgraded to a critical section
- The CPU raises a HardFault
> That's why data shared with ISRs uses critical sections (disable interrupts) or lock-free structures, not spin locks.

? What does the `CriticalSection<'cs>` token in `critical_section::Mutex::borrow` achieve?
- It disables interrupts when created
* It proves at compile time that you are inside a critical section, and its lifetime keeps the reference from escaping it
- It counts how many times the mutex was locked
- It makes the mutex re-entrant
> Only `critical_section::with` can create a token, and `borrow(&'cs self, cs: CriticalSection<'cs>) -> &'cs T` ties the data's lifetime to the critical section.

? What's the capacity of a classic SPSC ring buffer with `N` slots?
- N
* N - 1
- N / 2
- N + 1
> One slot stays empty so that `head == tail` can mean "empty" unambiguously.

? On `thumbv6m-none-eabi` (Cortex-M0), which of these is NOT available natively?
- `AtomicU32::load`
- `AtomicU32::store`
* `AtomicU32::fetch_add`
- `AtomicBool::store`
> ARMv6-M has no exclusive load/store instructions, so no compare-and-swap or read-modify-write atomics. Use `portable-atomic` (critical-section based) or design around load/store.

? For `unsafe impl<T> Sync for SpinLock<T>`, what bound on `T` is correct?
- `T: Sync`
* `T: Send`
- `T: Copy`
- no bound is needed
> The lock gives one thread at a time `&mut T`, effectively moving access between threads, which requires `Send`. `Sync` isn't needed because accesses never overlap. (`std::sync::Mutex<T>` has the same `T: Send` bound.)

? Which `Ordering` is sufficient for a statistics counter that's only ever incremented and read for display?
* `Relaxed`
- `Release`
- `Acquire`
- `SeqCst` is required for counters
> The counter itself is atomic with any ordering. Ordering only matters when the atomic is used to publish *other* memory.

? Why must interrupt-disabling critical sections be kept short?
- They consume flash
* All interrupts are delayed while they run, adding to worst-case interrupt latency
- They can't contain function calls
- They prevent the use of atomics
> Real-time guarantees depend on bounded interrupt latency; a long critical section delays every interrupt in the system.
